 ## Operation
 
+Qwen3.5-core requests compare lookup-copy yield with their measured neural draft yield.
+`ninfer-serve --ngram-arbitration on|off` controls this gate and defaults to `on`.
+The first two completed copy rounds establish an estimate. Later matches run when their
+estimated accepted-token count exceeds 1.3 times the neural round's expected committed tokens;
+after eight skipped matches, one probe is allowed. Rejecting a match also discards its archive
+continuation cursor. Estimates reset for each request, and target verification still decides
+which tokens are accepted. `off` retains direct lookup-copy admission for comparisons.
+
+The gate changes proposal selection, so it does not guarantee identical generated text or a
+speedup on every workload. It adds CPU request state and leaves the draft KV representation
+unchanged. The setting applies to the Qwen3.5-core MTP, DFlash and DFlash2 paths; it does not
+change other model families' lookup policies.
+
 Each request owns a bounded CPU index of source positions. Lookups use 16-, 8- and 4-token
 suffixes, compare hits exactly and extend matches backward through up to 64 committed
 tokens to select a source. A proposal
